ID: B2-Ch09b-Inseparable
TEXT:

 "Oh, they were a lot more than allies," Melindra says. "Six hundred years ago, they were practically inseparable. You would have taken them for a couple at a glance, if one hadn't been human and the other a fox spirit from the magical plane."

Inseparable. That sheds a different light on Eiden's fury back when I asked if he burned Olmnar to earn the fox's forgiveness.

"Huh," Hadrik says, scratching his beard. "Well. The more you know."

"Melindra, you skipped the funniest part!" Arraka's voice rattles from the golden amulet at Flower's chest.

"What are you talking about?" Melindra's tone turns sharp.

"How the fox has been stuck inside her sacred woods for decades over a lovers' quarrel with Eiden!" Arraka cackles.

"Why?" Daren asks. "Did Eiden seal her in with a ward?"

"It has nothing to do with Eiden's wards," Melindra says. "It's the dragon. Whenever she crosses out of her sacred groves, her power drops. Tyrath knows it. He'd tear her apart the second she stepped past the tree line."

 "Except," Arraka chimes in, "back when the fox and Eiden were still playing house, Tyrath wouldn't dare touch her. Step outside or not, Eiden would have crossed the continent in a single step and severed the lizard's head before his jaws closed."

"Wait," I say. "That's the only thing keeping the dragon off her? Couldn't he send squads to tie Eiden down while he went after the fox?"

"No," Melindra says flatly. "A few strike teams wouldn't pull Eiden's attention for a fraction of a second. She matters too much to him. To drag Eiden away from her, you'd need an entire army. Or a god."

"Hold on," Hadrik says. "Didn't the three of them sign a non-aggression pact? Why would Tyrath risk a full brawl with Eiden just to kill the fox?"

 "Because the prize is worth the blood!" Arraka snaps. "The only reason Tyrath signed that treaty was because he couldn't fight both of them together. Killing her leaves one enemy. And the oversized lizard is delusional enough to think he could take Eiden in a duel without reducing the continent to ash in the process."

"You don't think he could?" Daren asks.

"Not a prayer," Arraka says. "Tyrath wouldn't last five minutes against Eiden cutting loose. Remember that aura spike in the arena? When I said he was finally breaking a sweat? I wasn't exaggerating. Eiden's deeper than he shows. I fought him in the Still Winter. Raw power alone, he matches the dragon blow for blow. Add in his loopholes and misdirections? He'd turn Tyrath into a rug before the dragon finished drawing breath."

choice(""If he really is that powerful, then why didn't he just kill Tyrath until now?"", B2-Ch09b-Reveal, v_current_scene = B2-Ch09b-Reveal, v_b2_ch9_tyrath = 0)
choice(""Why does it sound to me like you're making up excuses for losing in the still winter?"", B2-Ch09b-Reveal, v_current_scene = B2-Ch09b-Reveal, v_b2_ch9_tyrath = 1)
choice(""But why would Eiden be so much more powerful than regular stillwaters?"", B2-Ch09b-Reveal, v_current_scene = B2-Ch09b-Reveal, v_b2_ch9_tyrath = 2)


ID: B2-Ch09b-Reveal
TEXT:

#if(v_b2_ch9_tyrath == 0) {
 "If he really is that powerful, then why didn't he just kill Tyrath until now?"

"The original treaty came with a ceasefire across all three factions," Melindra says. "And Eiden wasn't always at the dragon's throat. They used to hold monthly councils—Eiden, the fox, and Tyrath, sitting down to handle border disputes and shared trade. I sat in on a few. The tone wasn't all that different from how they talk now, but there was actual mutual respect back then. Deep down, even if he'd never admit it, Eiden would hate to see the dragon dead."

"Hah!" Arraka snorts. "Sentimentality? Eiden keeps the lizard around because he finds him amusing. Nothing more."

"Believe what you want," Melindra says, lifting a shoulder. "It's been centuries since I had an honest word with Eiden. His head isn't an easy place to read anymore."

}
#if(v_b2_ch9_tyrath == 1) {
 "Why does it sound to me like you're making up excuses for losing in the still winter?"

"Are you deaf, half-lessathi?" Arraka snaps. "Eiden's reserves are smaller than mine. He caught me off guard with parlor tricks. He wouldn't get a second chance."

"Right," I tell her. "Naturally."

}
#if(v_b2_ch9_tyrath == 2) {
 "But why would Eiden be so much more powerful than regular stillwaters?"

"How should I know?" Arraka says. "By the time I fused with Therius, the Stillwaters were already butcher-deep in war with the lessathi. The mechanics of their creation didn't interest me then, and they don't now."

"Right," I say.

}
 "You've been remarkably generous with Eiden's history today, Melindra," Illuna says, her blue eyes fixing on the stillwater. "What brought about this sudden wave of openness?"

"Well, since I'm so clearly Eiden's planted spy," Melindra draws out the words with heavy irony, "I can't keep every card hidden. I have to drop a few state secrets here and there, just to win your fragile hearts."

She shifts her gaze straight to Kate, a smirk playing on her lips.

"Wouldn't you agree?"

Kate's jaw tightens. She meets the look without a word.

"Right, maybe we steer away from the fireworks," Hadrik breaks in, stepping squarely between their lines of sight. "Speaking of weird things out here—anyone notice how quiet these woods are? I expected birds, deer, normal forest game. Instead it's just roaming mages and monsters trying to kill us."

choice("Continue", B2-Ch09b-Circles, v_current_scene = B2-Ch09b-Circles)


ID: B2-Ch09b-Circles
TEXT:

 "Most of the wildlife headed for the sacred groves as soon as the tournament started," Flower says. "They stay under the golden fox's wards until the fighting blows over. The continent's protection spell shields Varathian citizens, but the animals have no defense against stray fireballs and arena beasts."

"Not all of them made it," Arraka notes with relish. "See that column of black smoke past the ridge? There's a bear and two wolves trapped in a ring of fire. They've been scrambling in circles for two minutes looking for a gap, but the wall's solid. Another couple of breaths and they'll be cooked through."

"What?" Flower gasps, hands flying to her mouth. "No!"

"Oh, yes. Best free show of the afternoon. Two silver pieces on the bear lasting longer?"

"Stop it!" Flower yells.

 "Touchy. No appreciation for the sporting life."

"Wait—there's a brush fire out there?" Daren squints through the translucent ice wall of Kate's dome, scanning the gray canopy for sparks. "How close is it? Will it jump toward us?"

"The rain's tapered to a drizzle, the lightning's still cracking, and the wind's whipping through the pines," Arraka says. "Prime burning conditions. But the draft is pushing the front eastward. Unless a strike hits the branch right above this dome, we're out of the line of fire."

"Good," Daren exhales. "Then we stay put until the storm passes."

"Stay put?" Flower turns on him, green eyes flashing. "There are animals dying in that fire! We have to put it out!"

"Risking our necks for wild beasts isn't on the itinerary," Daren says firmly. "That's the fox's domain, not ours."

"Daren is right," Kate adds, her voice cool and steady. "We can't afford reckless detours in the middle of a storm."

"I don't believe this!" Flower stomps a bare foot against the ice floor. "Petal and I are going. With or without you!"

choice("Volunteer to go with Flower", B2-Ch09b-Save, v_current_scene = B2-Ch09b-Save, v_b2_ch9_animals = 1)
choice("Stay silent", B2-Ch09b-Silent, v_current_scene = B2-Ch09b-Silent, v_b2_ch9_animals = 0)


ID: B2-Ch09b-Silent
TEXT:

 "Flower, stand down—" Daren begins.

Flower doesn't wait. A concentrated flare of heat melts an arch straight through Kate's ice dome, slush hissing against the damp ground.

"Are you even listening?" Daren calls after her as she slips through the gap.

Flower pivots at the threshold. For an instant it looks like she's about to argue, but her gaze bypasses Daren entirely and lands on Melindra.

"Melindra! Move your feet! We're losing time!"

"Me?" Melindra blinks, pointing at her own chest.

"Yes, you! Hurry!"

"Fine, fine," Melindra groans, trailing after the red-haired girl with an exaggerated roll of her eyes. "Going on a forest rescue. Splendid."

"Watch for falling timber!" Daren shouts into the drizzle as Kate reseals the arch with fresh frost. "And keep clear of lightning strikes!"

"We know!" Flower's voice carries over the rising wind before both of them vanish into the pine thicket.

 "Should we really let them run off alone?" Hadrik asks, leaning against the curved wall.

"Flower and Illuna have kept themselves alive for twenty years without our supervision," Kate says quietly. "A patch of burning brush won't finish them."

"Fair enough," Hadrik grunts.

"If they aren't back in twenty minutes, we move," Daren says, pacing the perimeter of the dome. "Though I'd rather not have to dig them out."

The minutes drag inside the frozen shell. The rain taps against the curved ceiling, slackening by degrees. Ten minutes in, just as Daren reaches for his sword hilt to call a search, two silhouettes break through the dripping pines.

"All clear!" Flower calls, waving both arms. "The fire's out and the storm is breaking!"

"No burns," Daren breathes a quiet sigh as Kate drops the dome, letting the frost dissolve into steam. "Good. I was preparing for third-degree healing. Did you find them?"

"They got out safe," Flower says, wiping soot from her forehead. "We can keep moving now."

"Then let's make tracks," Daren says, gesturing south. "Preferably away from whatever timber is still smoldering."

choice("Continue", B2-Ch09b-Leila, v_current_scene = B2-Ch09b-Leila)


ID: B2-Ch09b-Save
TEXT:

 "I'm coming with you," I say, stepping up beside Flower.

"Barry, be reasonable!" Daren protests. "They're wild animals!"

"They talk," I say. "That puts them a step above wild."

"They're still beasts!" Daren snaps.

"Come on, Barry!" Flower says, channeling heat into the wall. An opening slumps open in a rush of steam.

"Right behind you," I say.

"Barry, wait—" Daren reaches out.

"Relax," I tell him over my shoulder. "I've survived mages, dragons, and arenas; a brush fire isn't taking me out. Illuna has water magic on tap. We'll douse the worst of it and pull them through."

Flower halts at the exit, glancing back at Melindra, who's still lounging against the ice.

"Melindra! What are you waiting for? We need to go!"

"Me?" Melindra blinks, tapping her chest.

 "Yes, you! Move!"

"Fine, fine," Melindra sighs, pushing off the wall. "Forest firefighting. My favorite pastime."

"Keep your heads down!" Daren calls out as Kate knits the frost back together behind us. "Watch the tree canopy for lightning strikes!"

"Understood!" I yell back, and we plunge into the storm, heading for the thick black smoke pluming above the pines.

We sprint through the sodden brush for a minute, Flower setting a blistering pace out front. The deluge has shrunk to a steady mist, but the gusts still tear through the high branches with a dull roar. At this clip, the burn line is barely a minute away.

"Surprised you tagged along," I say to Melindra, matching her stride over slick roots. "I had the impression you weren't fond of the golden fox's menagerie."

"I couldn't care less about the animals," Melindra says without breaking stride. "Flower asked. That's the only reason I'm here."

"Fair enough," I say.

choice("Continue", B2-Ch09b-Save2, v_current_scene = B2-Ch09b-Save2)


ID: B2-Ch09b-Save2
TEXT:

 "Heads up!" Flower calls, slowing at the edge of a burning clearing. "Arraka, where are they pinned down?"

"I could tell you," Arraka's voice floats from the amulet, "but that would spoil the thrill of the hunt, wouldn't it?"

"Typical," Flower scowls. "Fine, we'll track them ourselves. Their auras should stand out once we clear the ridge."

"Over that way," Melindra cuts in, pointing northeast toward a wall of burning timber. "Three distinct pulses. I have range too, in case you forgot."

"Right, thank you, Melindra!" Flower beams. "Let's move!"

A thick bank of choking black smoke hangs across the slope, sealing off the burning grove like a heavy curtain.

"Melindra, can you scatter this smoke with wind?" Flower asks, coughing slightly.

Melindra freezes, staring into the roiling soot. Her shoulders lock up. Her wind control is volatile at the best of times, and a misjudged gust here could whip the fire into an uncontrollable firestorm.

 "Melindra?" Flower asks, her voice dropping.

"She needs a clear radius to channel without catching us in the backdraft," I tell Flower, taking her shoulder. "Step back with me. Give her room."

Melindra catches my eye, giving a brief, taut nod of relief.

Once we're twenty paces back, she plants her boots and releases a sharp, focused cone of pressurized air. The draft blows the smoke apart in a violent outward shockwave, exposing the flaming ring beyond. The instant the line of sight clears, Illuna takes over the shared body—eyes snapping to vivid sapphire—and unleashes twin torrents of water from her palms, drowning the nearest burning pines in a roar of steam and falling embers.

"Path is open," Flower's voice returns. "Go!"

We scramble through the wet ash. Beyond the charred perimeter, three auras register in close proximity. As we round a burning stump, two massive gray wolves drop into a crouch, lips peeling back over white fangs with low, rattling growls. Behind them, a towering brown bear rears onto its hind legs, claws out, casting a massive shadow through the smoke.

choice("Continue", B2-Ch09b-Failed, v_current_scene = B2-Ch09b-Failed, v_b2_ch9_animals = 1) if (v_ancient_languages < 2)
choice("Continue", B2-Ch09b-Success, v_current_scene = B2-Ch09b-Success, v_b2_ch9_animals = 2) if (v_ancient_languages >= 2)


ID: B2-Ch09b-Failed
TEXT:

"Hold on," Flower tells us, stepping forward with palms open. "I know some of their dialect. Let me speak to them."

She produces a sequence of chittering growls and low clicks, gesturing toward the sodden breach we carved through the perimeter. The wolves' ears twitch back; their snarls drop to wary rumbles.

"Step aside," Flower whispers. "Give them room to run."

We back against the smoking tree trunks, clearing a wide lane. The wolves exchange a glance with the towering bear, then lower their hackles and slip past us at a steady lope, not looking back.

"I'm not sure how much grammar carried over," Flower admits, watching them retreat, "but they understood the exit route. Let's give them a head start before we head back to the dome."

We linger near the perimeter until the rustle of underbrush fades into the distance. The storm has wound down to scattered droplets, the wind dropping to a gentle breeze through the scorched pines. We make our way back through the wet forest, finding the rest of the group waiting inside Kate's translucent dome.

"We're back!" Flower calls out. "The fire's clear and the storm is done!"

"No burns," Daren says, letting out a breath as Kate dismisses the ice, letting the frost dissolve into steam. "Good. You took long enough that I was bracing for skin grafts. Did you find them?"

"They got out safe," Flower says cheerfully. "We can keep moving now."

"Then let's make tracks," Daren says, gesturing south. "Preferably away from whatever timber is still smoldering."

choice("Continue", B2-Ch09b-Leila, v_current_scene = B2-Ch09b-Leila)


ID: B2-Ch09b-Success
TEXT:

"Hold," I call out in the guttural cadence of the ancient beast tongue. "We came to get you out."

The lead wolf's ears prick up. "How did you breach the perimeter?" he demands in the same tongue. "We have been running this circle for minutes. The flame has no break."

"We brought a water caster," I answer. "What are you doing this far from the sacred woods? The tournament grounds are crawling with mages."

"The golden fox dispatched us on a search," the second wolf says.

"Silence, brother!" the first snaps. "The mission is not for strangers."

"They drowned the fire to reach us," the second retorts. "The least we owe them is honesty."

"What did she send you to find?" I ask. "If it's not a secret, we might have seen it."

"A ward," the second wolf says. "The fox sensed an unfamiliar casting in these woods carrying the signature of the stillwater, Eiden. My brother and I were chosen for our tracking scents; the bear is our guard. You haven't crossed a faint, buried spell on your path, have you?"

choice("Tell him about Eiden's spell.", B2-Ch09b-Spell, v_current_scene = B2-Ch09b-Spell, v_b2_ch9_spell = 1)
choice("Don't tell him about Eiden's spell.", B2-Ch09b-Spell, v_current_scene = B2-Ch09b-Spell, v_b2_ch9_spell = 0)


ID: B2-Ch09b-Spell
TEXT:

set(v_ac_b2_ch9_helper,1) if (v_b2_ch9_spell == 1)
achievement("I'm a helper!",v_ac_b2_ch9_helper)
#if(v_b2_ch9_spell == 1) {
 "We crossed it this morning," I tell him. "Head due east for roughly three hours until you hit the riverbed. Follow the current downstream another hour, and you'll catch the pulse if your tracking is sharp."

"You have our thanks," the second wolf says, bowing his head slightly. "We have nothing of value on us to repay you, but if you pass near the sacred borders, send word. Wolves keep their debts."

"Understood," I say. "Get moving before the wind shifts the flames back onto your trail. The gap is behind us."

"Farewell, human," the wolf answers. "Come, brother. Out of this ash."

"I didn't know you were fluent in beast tongue, Barry!" Flower says as the three animals bound toward the gap. "I only caught fragments of that. What was the fox's errand?"

"She sent them hunting for the buried spell of Eiden's we stumbled on earlier," I say. "I gave them the coordinates."

"Good," Flower nods. "Let's give them a minute to clear the perimeter before we head back."

We wait until the heavy padding of paws fades into the pines, then retrace our steps through the soggy ash. The storm has died down to a gentle mist, the dark underbelly of the clouds rolling away toward the horizon. By the time we reach Kate's dome, the gusts have faded completely.

"We're back!" Flower calls out. "The fire's clear and the storm is done!"

"No burns," Daren says with evident relief as Kate drops the dome, letting the frost dissolve into steam. "I was starting to worry I'd be treating charred flesh. Did the rescue go well?"

"Everyone's safe," Flower says with a smile. "We can hit the road."

"Then let's make tracks," Daren says. "Southward, away from the smoke."

}
#if(v_b2_ch9_spell == 0) {
 "Nothing of the sort," I tell him. "My magical sense isn't sharp enough to catch faint signatures on the road."

"We appreciate the rescue regardless," the second wolf says, lowering his snout. "We carry nothing to reward you with here, but if your travels take you near the sacred borders, call for our pack. Wolves keep their debts."

"Understood," I say. "Get moving before the wind turns the embers back on you. The gap is behind us."

"Farewell, human," the wolf answers. "Come, brother. Out of this ash."

"I didn't know you were fluent in beast tongue, Barry!" Flower says as the three animals bound toward the gap. "I only caught fragments of that. What was the fox's errand?"

"The fox sent them hunting for that hidden casting of Eiden's from this morning," I say. "I kept it quiet and told them we hadn't seen anything."

"I see," Flower says. "Let's give them a minute to clear the perimeter before we head back."

We wait until the heavy padding of paws fades into the pines, then retrace our steps through the soggy ash. The storm has died down to a gentle mist, the dark underbelly of the clouds rolling away toward the horizon. By the time we reach Kate's dome, the gusts have faded completely.

"We're back!" Flower calls out. "The fire's clear and the storm is done!"

"No burns," Daren says with evident relief as Kate drops the dome, letting the frost dissolve into steam. "I was starting to worry I'd be treating charred flesh. Did the rescue go well?"

"Everyone's safe," Flower says with a smile. "We can hit the road."

"Then let's make tracks," Daren says. "Southward, away from the smoke."

}
choice("Continue", B2-Ch09b-Leila, v_current_scene = B2-Ch09b-Leila)


ID: B2-Ch09b-Leila
TEXT:

The damp pine forest slowly gives way to rolling hillocks of silver-tipped grass as the afternoon wears on. A few lone mages harvest enchanted pinecones in the high branches, watching our formation from a wary distance before retreating into the canopy. With bruised clouds still brooding on the western horizon, Daren calls an early halt in a sheltered hollow, pitching tents before another squall can flatten our canvas.

Hadrik immediately corners the camp for a dwarven tavern card game. I wave my spell notebook as an ironclad excuse, but Kate and Melindra have no such cover. Both try repeatedly to decline, yet Hadrik's booming insistence wears them down until they're seated cross-legged on the turf across from each other. His scheme is transparent: force them into the same circle until some form of peace takes root. Nobody calls him out on it, and the game proceeds under a cloud of stiff, competitive silence.

A few lines of luminous blue script coalesce in the twilight air above my open page.

"Still hunting loopholes?" Leila writes.

"Always," I answer, looking up from my notes. "They don't uncover themselves. Weren't you in the middle of Hadrik's match?"

"The rules require shouting card names the moment they hit the grass," Leila's glowing text forms beside her shoulder. "Floating letters have a half-second lag. I was losing every hand. Mind if I sit?"

"Go right ahead," I tell her, gesturing to the dry patch beside me. She settles down onto the turf, tucking her legs beneath her.

choice(""So, how have things been going with Kate?"", B2-Ch09b-Things, v_current_scene = B2-Ch09b-Things)
choice(""So, how do you like our little group, so far?"", B2-Ch09b-Group, v_current_scene = B2-Ch09b-Group)
choice(""So, what have you been up to, lately?"", B2-Ch09b-Lately, v_current_scene = B2-Ch09b-Lately)


ID: B2-Ch09b-Things
TEXT:

 "So, how have things been going with Kate?"

"Better than I dared to hope," glowing script hovers as a slight smile touches her lips. "We barely spoke at the Beacon. I never imagined she would consider me a friend now. I'm glad I was wrong."

"She has opened up around you these last couple of days," I say. "Kate usually keeps her walls high. It's rare to see her relax like that."

"She wasn't always this guarded," Leila writes. "Back at the Beacon, before the worst of it, she talked constantly with Diane. Maybe having people she can genuinely rely on is bringing that side of her back."

"Makes sense," I say. "This might be the first time in ten years she hasn't had to sleep with one eye on her allies' knives."

"Do you think I could manage that too, someday?" the blue script asks.

"Manage what?"

"Become part of your group..."

"What are you talking about? You're already with us."

Leila shakes her head gently.

"Only on loan," the text spells out. "When Ollendor is settled, I stay behind with my father. You and the others will move on to the next trial."

"We won't be on the road forever either," I tell her. "When the tournament wraps up, the group scatters, but that doesn't mean we sever ties. Whenever we cross paths again, there's always an open spot for you."

"Crossing paths after you leave Varathia won't be simple," she writes.

"Varathian citizens can sponsor outsider travel," I point out. "An official invitation from you clears the gate easily enough."

choice("Continue", B2-Ch09b-Breakthrough, v_current_scene = B2-Ch09b-Breakthrough)


ID: B2-Ch09b-Group
TEXT:

 "So, how do you like our little group, so far?"

"It has its charm," Leila writes, a quiet smile forming. "Hadrik's volume can be exhausting, and Daren has a habit of lecturing, but they're good people. I enjoy traveling with them."

"They grow on you," I say. "Give it time and you won't even blink at the quirks. Even Arraka becomes background noise eventually. Spend enough weeks dodging lethal spells together and you stop thinking of them as travel companions and start seeing them as family."

"Do you think I could manage that too, someday?" she writes.

"Manage what?"

"Become part of your group..."

"What are you talking about? You're already with us."

Leila shakes her head gently.

"Only on loan," the text spells out. "When Ollendor is settled, I stay behind with my father. You and the others will move on to the next trial."

"We won't be on the road forever either," I tell her. "When the tournament wraps up, the group scatters, but that doesn't mean we sever ties. Whenever we cross paths again, there's always an open spot for you."

"Crossing paths after you leave Varathia won't be simple," she writes.

"Varathian citizens can sponsor outsider travel," I point out. "An official invitation from you clears the gate easily enough."

choice("Continue", B2-Ch09b-Breakthrough, v_current_scene = B2-Ch09b-Breakthrough)


ID: B2-Ch09b-Lately
TEXT:

 "So, what have you been up to, lately?"

"Hadrik appointed me permanent referee for his contests with Daren," Leila's letters gleam in the fading light. "And now he wants me to project live scoreboards during tavern card matches. I seem to be accumulating administrative duties."

"Promotion through logistics," I chuckle. "Keep impressing the squad and I'll issue your official membership credentials."

"Do you think I could earn that, someday?" she writes.

"Earn what?"

"A real place in your group..."

"What are you talking about? You're already with us."

Leila shakes her head gently.

"Only on loan," the text spells out. "When Ollendor is settled, I stay behind with my father. You and the others will move on to the next trial."

"We won't be on the road forever either," I tell her. "When the tournament wraps up, the group scatters, but that doesn't mean we sever ties. Whenever we cross paths again, there's always an open spot for you."

"Crossing paths after you leave Varathia won't be simple," she writes.

"Varathian citizens can sponsor outsider travel," I point out. "An official invitation from you clears the gate easily enough."

choice("Continue", B2-Ch09b-Breakthrough, v_current_scene = B2-Ch09b-Breakthrough)


ID: B2-Ch09b-Breakthrough
TEXT:

 "Invitations require clean records," Leila writes. "Father and I are registered outlaws. Ollendor's rogue status means the other kingdoms reject our paperwork on principle anyway."

"That assumes the king survives our visit," I say. "A successful assassination rewrites the local politics. Plus, Golmyck owes us a few favors. Once his faction gains traction in Thilias, wiping a few names from a ledger won't be out of the question. Have a little optimism."

"Perhaps," the blue text wavers thoughtfully. "At minimum, Hadrik won't leave the island without a massive farewell feast. We'll have one last gathering before the ships sail."

"See? You already have the team pegged," I tell her. "Two days on the road and you're thinking like a veteran. You're practically on the roster."

"Then I will work hard to keep my standing," she writes with a genuine grin.

"Hey, Leila!" Hadrik's roar cuts across the grass. "We need our scoreboard back! We'll tweak the callout timer so your floating script can compete!"

"Duty calls," Leila's letters drift as she stands. "Good luck with the spell diagrams, Barry."

"Don't let them hustle you out of points," I tell her.

"Never," she writes.

She turns toward the shouting circle, glowing letters leading her way:

"Deal me in!"

#if(v_b2_ch8_energizer == 1) {
I spend the next twenty minutes wrestling with arcane theory, trying to tune out the shouts and laughter from the card game. The notes stubbornly refuse to yield any new angles. Searching for hidden loopholes through brute-force reading is hitting a wall. There has to be another avenue of approach, but every thread I pull comes up tangled.

A sharp throb behind my temples shatters my train of thought, followed by a sour heave in my stomach. The second energizer spell has expired, dumping hours of accumulated fatigue straight into my skull. Pushing through complex theory in this state is impossible. I drop back onto the grass, eyes shut, waiting for the spinning to slow.

Ten minutes into the ache, a sharp burst of static crackles from the transceiver in my pocket, followed by a voice that snaps my eyes wide open.

}
#if(v_b2_ch8_energizer != 1) {
I spend the next half hour digging through the notebook, tuning out the shouts and banter from Hadrik's table. The spell diagrams stubbornly refuse to yield any new angles. Searching for hidden loopholes through brute-force reading is hitting a wall. There has to be another avenue of approach, but every thread I pull comes up tangled.

As I lean back against the pack to clear my head, a burst of static crackles from the transceiver at my hip, followed by a voice that snaps my spine straight.

}
choice("Continue", B2-Ch09b-Revenant, v_current_scene = B2-Ch09b-Revenant) if (v_ch11_saved_rose == 0)
choice("Continue", B2-Ch09b-Freezing, v_current_scene = B2-Ch09b-Freezing) if (v_ch11_saved_rose == 1)


ID: B2-Ch09b-Revenant
TEXT:

#if(v_b2_ch8_energizer == 3) {
 "Hello?" Rose's voice drifts through the speaker. "Is this channel still open?"

Hearing Rose's voice unexpectedly like this makes my heart skip a beat. Seeing that there is no response from my side, the voice calls out once more.

"Are you hearing this?"

Breathe, Barry. That isn't Rose. You know what's speaking through that bronze casing. It's the revenant, holding the transceiver Daren handed to Rose back on our second day of the tournament. I tap the rune once so she can hear me, keeping my voice level.

 "What do you want?" I say.

"So you can hear me, then," the revenant says. "Good. Make sure to leave this channel open. I will be contacting you again soon. Don't try to call me. I will be keeping this channel closed on my side until I have a reason to call you. Goodbye."

"Wait—" I demand, but the connection snaps shut.

The channel on her side is now closed.

}
#if(v_b2_ch8_energizer != 3) {
 "Hello?" Rose's voice drifts through the speaker. "Is this channel still open?"

Hearing Rose's voice out of nowhere drives straight into my chest like a dagger. I have spent every waking hour since our toast with Enrique avoiding any thought of Rose's death. Promising myself I would not run from her memory was easy; the reality is an entirely different matter.

"Are you hearing this?" Rose's voice says again.

Breathe, Barry. That isn't Rose. You know what's speaking through that bronze casing. It's the revenant, holding the transceiver Daren handed to Rose back on our second day of the tournament. I tap the rune once so she can hear me, keeping my voice level.

 "What do you want?" I say.

"So you can hear me, then," the revenant says. "Good. Make sure to leave this channel open. I will be contacting you again soon. Don't try to call me. I will be keeping this channel closed on my side until I have a reason to call you. Goodbye."

"Wait—" I demand, but the connection snaps shut.

The channel on her side is now closed.

}
choice("Keep the channel open on my side.", B2-Ch09b-Channel, v_current_scene = B2-Ch09b-Channel, v_b2_ch9_channel = 1)
choice("Close the channel on my side too.", B2-Ch09b-Channel, v_current_scene = B2-Ch09b-Channel, v_b2_ch9_channel = 0)


ID: B2-Ch09b-Channel
TEXT:

#if(v_b2_ch9_channel == 1) {
 I leave the channel open on my side. Whatever the revenant is planning, shutting down our only line of intelligence would be reckless.

"Was that... who I think it was?" Daren asks, the rest of the circle trailing close behind him.

"The revenant," I say. "She still has your old transceiver."

"What did she want?" Daren asks.

"She was checking if the line was active," I answer. "She ordered me to keep the channel open for future contact, gave no reason, and cut the transmission before I could press her for answers."

"Huh..." Hadrik says, tossing his cards onto the grass. "Well, that puts an end to the card table for me. Let's pack them up for tonight, and we'll finish the game another time."

}
#if(v_b2_ch9_channel == 0) {
 I close the channel on my side as well. I have no obligation to dance on a revenant's strings.

"Was that... who I think it was?" Daren asks, the rest of the circle trailing close behind him.

"The revenant," I say. "She still has your old transceiver."

"What did she want?" Daren asks.

"She was checking if the line was active," I answer. "She ordered me to keep the channel open, refused to explain why, and severed the line on her end. So I closed ours as well and called it a day."

"Probably for the best," Daren murmurs.

"Huh..." Hadrik says, tossing his cards onto the grass. "Well, that puts an end to the card table for me. Let's pack them up for tonight, and we'll finish the game another time."

}
choice("Continue", B2-Ch09b-Shift, v_current_scene = B2-Ch09b-Shift)


ID: B2-Ch09b-Freezing
TEXT:

#if(v_b2_ch8_energizer == 1) {
 "Barry?" Rose says. "Can you hear me?"

"Yeah, I hear you," I say, pushing upright while forcing my pounding head to focus. I tap the transceiver through my pocket so she can hear me.

"Was that Rose I just heard?" Hadrik shouts from across the grass.

"On the line!" I shout back, holding up the device.

"Alright, let's take a break," Hadrik says, leading the rest of the circle toward my spot.

"Barry, is everyone else there with you?" Rose asks.

"Gathering around right now," I tell her, breathing through the ache in my temples. "Hold on a second."

I wait for the others to gather around before speaking again.

"Everyone is here," I tell Rose, keeping my voice calm against the headache. "What happened?"

}
#if(v_b2_ch8_energizer != 1) {
 "Barry?" Rose says. "Can you hear me?"

"Yeah, I hear you," I say, tapping the transceiver through my pocket so she can hear me.

"Was that Rose I just heard?" Hadrik shouts from across the grass.

"On the line!" I shout back, holding up the device.

"Alright, let's take a break," Hadrik says, leading the rest of the circle toward my spot.

"Barry, is everyone else there with you?" Rose asks.

"Gathering around right now," I tell her. "Hold on a second."

I wait for the others to gather around before speaking again.

"Everyone is here," I tell Rose. "What happened?"

}
 "I'm... not sure how to explain it," Rose says, her voice strained and thin. "Something terrifying happened a few minutes ago. I was walking around the house, when everything locked in place. I tried calling out to my siblings, but they wouldn't answer, they wouldn't breathe, they were just standing there, looking straight ahead, while even the leaves falling from the trees were floating in mid-air, completely frozen. And there was nothing I could do. Time eventually unfroze on its own, but I have no idea why, or if it will happen again. I must have activated my time powers by mistake, with absolutely no control over them. I'm terrified that next time I won't be able to undo the spell, and I'll be trapped forever in a world where everyone is frozen in time except for me..."

"Oh, I wouldn't worry too much about that," Melindra cuts in from the edge of the circle.

"What?..." Daren asks. "Why shouldn't she?"

"Because no time-freezing spell lasts forever," Melindra says. "The mana runs out eventually. But if you don't feel like waiting and lack a direct counter-spell, try to physically interact with some of the people who are frozen in time. Every time-freezing spell shares the same vulnerability: you cannot physically touch living beings frozen in time without triggering the cancellation of the spell."

 "That's..." Rose pauses, a breath of pure relief trembling across the link. "Thank you! I will try that the next time I trigger a time-freezing spell by accident."

"Tell me," Melindra says, tilting her head. "You wouldn't by any chance happen to be a time weaver, would you?"

"I am," Rose says softly. "Or at least that is what Eiden called me when he first saw me."

"Oh?" Melindra muses. "How interesting..."

"Should we take it that you've met a time weaver before, Melindra?" Daren asks.

"I did," Melindra says. "During the Still Winter. And as I said—"

"Right, of course," Daren cuts in with a wave. "You told Eiden that you wouldn't talk to anyone about the Still Winter. We get it."

choice(""Could you at least tell us what you know about time weavers in general?"", B2-Ch09b-Energizer-Rose, v_current_scene = B2-Ch09b-Energizer-Rose, v_b2_ch9_weavers = 1) if (v_b2_ch8_energizer == 1)
choice(""Rose, do you remember what you were doing before the time froze?"", B2-Ch09b-Energizer-Rose, v_current_scene = B2-Ch09b-Energizer-Rose, v_b2_ch9_weavers = 0) if (v_b2_ch8_energizer == 1)
choice(""Could you at least tell us what you know about time weavers in general?"", B2-Ch09b-Weaver, v_current_scene = B2-Ch09b-Weaver, v_b2_ch9_weavers = 1) if (v_b2_ch8_energizer != 1)
choice(""Rose, do you remember what you were doing before the time froze?"", B2-Ch09b-Weaver, v_current_scene = B2-Ch09b-Weaver, v_b2_ch9_weavers = 0) if (v_b2_ch8_energizer != 1)


ID: B2-Ch09b-Energizer-Rose
TEXT:

#if(v_b2_ch9_weavers == 1) {
 "Could you at least tell us what you know about-- Ugh..."

A sudden wave of nausea hits my gut, cutting off my voice. I swallow hard against the bile, but the sweat on my face gives me away.

"Barry... is something wrong?" Rose asks.

"The energizer spell just ran out again," Melindra notes with a shrug. "I'd brace for an avalanche of fatigue to hit in the next few minutes."

"Wait, energizer spell?" Rose asks. "What is she talking about?"

}
#if(v_b2_ch9_weavers == 0) {
 "Rose, do you remember what you were doing before-- Ugh..."

A sudden wave of nausea hits my gut, cutting off my voice. I swallow hard against the bile, but the sweat on my face gives me away.

"Barry... is something wrong?" Rose asks.

"The energizer spell just ran out again," Melindra notes with a shrug. "I'd brace for an avalanche of fatigue to hit in the next few minutes."

"Wait, energizer spell?" Rose asks. "What is she talking about?"

}
#if(v_ch11_romance_rose == 0) {
 "Melindra spent the night in Barry's tent yesterday," Kate's voice cuts in, clipped and frigid. "They both used an energizer spell, but Barry has never used one before, so he is experiencing fatigue-induced headaches and nausea."

"Oh," Rose says softly. "I see..."

"So, uh..." Hadrik clears his throat loudly, trying to steer the topic. "How are things going in Thilias?"

}
#if(v_ch11_romance_rose == 1) {
 "Melindra spent the night in Barry's tent yesterday," Kate's voice cuts in, clipped and frigid. "They both used an energizer spell, but Barry has never used one before, so he is experiencing fatigue-induced headaches and nausea. I think you can imagine what they used the energizer spell for."

"Yes... I understand," Rose says. Her voice tries for composure, but the hurt bleeds through every syllable.

"I'm sorry," Kate says quietly.

"There is no reason to be sorry," Rose says. "What happened between me and Barry was only for one night. There were no commitments. Right, Barry?..."

The tone of her voice carries a faint, desperate edge, pleading with me to let this go. Any defense I offer now will only make things worse. Sometimes the cleanest path is taking the blow.

"Yes," I tell her, the word heavy in my throat. "You're right."

A long silence settles over the circle, nobody meeting anyone else's eyes.

"So, uh..." Hadrik says, trying to break the tension. "How are things going in Thilias?"

}
choice("Continue", B2-Ch09b-Thilias, v_current_scene = B2-Ch09b-Thilias)


ID: B2-Ch09b-Weaver
TEXT:

#if(v_b2_ch9_weavers == 1) {
 "Could you at least tell us what you know about time weavers in general?"

"There's no universal manual on them," Melindra says. "Most of what I know comes from the one time weaver I met, and as I said, the specifics stay buried. One thing is fairly certain, though: your friend's powers will likely awaken fully soon."

"Will she be able to control them?" I ask.

"That will depend on her," Melindra says.

"Hmm..." I mutter.

"Let's not pile more anxiety on her shoulders," Daren steps in. "Rose has practical advice on how to break the time-freezing spells, so let's leave it at that for now."

"Agreed," Hadrik says. "So, how are things going in Thilias?"

}
#if(v_b2_ch9_weavers == 0) {
 "Rose, do you remember what you were doing before the time froze?"

"I... wasn't doing anything out of the ordinary," Rose says. "I finished cooking and was carrying the food to my siblings in the yard."

"Did you feel any surge of strong emotion right before the spell triggered?" I ask.

"No," Rose answers. "I was simply carrying the plates. Nothing unusual happened before the freeze."

"Hmm..." I say.

"Let's not pile more anxiety on her shoulders," Daren steps in. "Rose has practical advice on how to break the time-freezing spells, so let's leave it at that for now."

"Agreed," Hadrik says. "So, how are things going in Thilias?"

}
choice("Continue", B2-Ch09b-Thilias, v_current_scene = B2-Ch09b-Thilias)


ID: B2-Ch09b-Thilias
TEXT:

set(v_ac_b2_ch9_king,1) if (v_b2_ch1_interfere == 0 && v_b2_ch2_deal == 3)
achievement("Long live the king!",v_ac_b2_ch9_king)
#if(v_b2_ch1_interfere == 1) {
 "There hasn't been much going on since you left," Rose says. "I suppose the biggest news is Master Venard announcing his retirement."

"The arena owner?" Daren asks.

"Yes," Rose says. "His retirement came as a shock to many. People say the arenas will not be the same without him. The reason he gave was that the latest arena event was the crowning achievement of his career, and having organized the greatest spectacle Thilias has ever witnessed, he wants to step down and leave the rest to the next generation."

"Huh..." Hadrik says. "Well, he would never get the chance to assemble that many rare beasts again in his lifetime. Anything else he staged from now on would feel dull by comparison. Can't blame him for walking away on a high note."

"Not to mention his stable of creatures was wiped out after the last event," Daren says, "so he likely wanted to avoid the hassle of procuring new beasts all over again. Thilias won't see another creature tournament for a long time."

"Exhibitions involving creatures have been suspended indefinitely," Rose says, "though gladiatorial bouts continue as scheduled. I'm not sure how many spectators will keep attending once they realize the beasts are gone, though..."

}
#if(v_b2_ch1_interfere == 0 && v_b2_ch2_deal != 3) {
 "They're not going well," Rose says, her tone darkening. "We found out today that Master Venard has been assassinated."

"The arena owner?" Daren asks. "Who killed him?"

"Nobody knows," Rose says. "Rumors are everywhere, but none seem credible. The town guards are still searching for the killer."

"They'll never find him," I say. "It was the lessathi. Venard disobeyed their direct orders to give Leila and me a chance in the arena, and the lessathi don't tolerate insubordination."

"That's terrible!" Rose says. "Why would he risk his life for an arena match?"

"He told us he didn't care what happened to him afterward," I say. "He wanted that final event to be perfect, and he wasn't going to let anyone ruin his ring. Not even the lessathi."

"I see..." Rose says softly.

}
#if(v_b2_ch1_interfere == 0 && v_b2_ch2_deal == 3) {
 "They have taken a terrible turn..." Rose says, her voice strained. "News reached us today that King Golmyck has passed away. The palace claims heart failure, but foul play is on everyone's lips. The whole city is in mourning. No new election has been called; rumor has it the nobility aims to place one of their own in power to prevent another populist like Golmyck from taking the throne."

"Golmyck was in perfect health when we saw him," Daren says grimly. "This smells like the work of the lessathi. Golmyck told us he was planning a summit with animal delegates to counter lessathi influence in the city. The lessathi must have caught wind of that meeting and struck first."

The realization drives straight into my chest. The acoustic bug I planted in Golmyck's soundproofed room—he must have hosted the animal delegates in that very chamber, unknowingly broadcasting his plans straight to the enemy. I am the one who got Golmyck killed.

"If that is true, they may have killed Master Venard as well..." Rose adds.

"The arena owner?" Daren asks. "He was murdered too?"

"Yes," Rose says. "His assassination was announced shortly before the king's death. The town guards have no credible leads."

"They'll never find the killer," I say, keeping my voice from shaking. "It was the lessathi. Venard disobeyed their direct orders to help Leila and me in the arena. He said he didn't care what happened to him afterward as long as his final show was flawless. He wasn't going to let anyone ruin it. Not even the lessathi."

"I see..." Rose murmurs.

}
 "Rooooose!" Suzie's voice calls shrilly through the transceiver. "Kevin is being mean to me again!"

"I'll be right there, Suzie!" Rose calls back before addressing us again.

"Sorry, I need to attend to the children," Rose says. "We'll speak again soon. Thank you for the help!"

We offer our goodbyes, and she closes the transceiver to go deal with her siblings.

"So, back to the game, then?" Hadrik asks.

"Let's finish the round we started and call it a night," Daren says. "I want to get some sleep before another storm blows in out of nowhere."

choice("Continue", B2-Ch09b-Shift, v_current_scene = B2-Ch09b-Shift)


ID: B2-Ch09b-Shift
TEXT:

#if(v_b2_ch8_energizer == 1) {
 Once Hadrik wraps up the final hand, the camp disperses to their bedrolls, Illuna taking the first perimeter watch. Daren allocates a spare canvas shelter to Melindra, sparing my tent any further late-night drama.

The first hour is a blur of dull throbbing and sour nausea, but the feverish edge gradually cools into deep exhaustion. Sleep takes me in pieces until Hadrik's heavy boot nudges the tent flap, his deep voice cutting through the dark.

"Rise and shine, Barry," Hadrik whispers loudly. "Watch duty. I brought a pair of dwarven ale bottles that won't drink themselves."

}
#if(v_b2_ch8_energizer == 0 || v_b2_ch8_energizer == 2) {
 Once Hadrik wraps up the final hand, the camp disperses to their bedrolls, Illuna taking the first perimeter watch. I make sure Daren sets up Melindra with a spare tent of her own, guaranteeing an undisturbed night.

Exhaustion claims me the moment my head hits the rolled cloak. Hours drift past until Hadrik's heavy boot nudges the tent flap, his deep voice cutting through the dark.

"Rise and shine, Barry," Hadrik whispers loudly. "Watch duty. I brought a pair of dwarven ale bottles that won't drink themselves."

}
#if(v_b2_ch8_energizer == 3) {
 Once Hadrik wraps up the final hand, the camp disperses to their bedrolls, Illuna taking the first perimeter watch. Daren allocates a spare canvas shelter to Melindra, sparing my tent any further late-night drama.

Exhaustion claims me the moment my head hits the rolled cloak. Hours drift past until Hadrik's heavy boot nudges the tent flap, his deep voice cutting through the dark.

"Rise and shine, Barry," Hadrik whispers loudly. "Watch duty. I brought a pair of dwarven ale bottles that won't drink themselves."

}
choice("Agree to drink with Hadrik", B2-Ch09b-Met-zek, v_current_scene = B2-Ch09b-Met-zek, v_b2_ch9_drink = 1)
choice("Tell him that I'd rather stay sober tonight", B2-Ch09b-Met-zek, v_current_scene = B2-Ch09b-Met-zek, v_b2_ch9_drink = 0)


ID: B2-Ch09b-Met-zek
TEXT:

#if(v_b2_ch9_drink == 1) {
 "I'll take a few sips," I say. "No full bottles. Dwarven brew hits my bloodstream like a warhammer, and I want my reflexes operational if scouts stumble into camp."

"Fair bargain," Hadrik grins. "Let's take the knoll."

After pulling on my gear and strapping on my weapons, I follow his stout frame out into the cool night air. We take up positions on a pair of mossy logs overlooking the tent circle, far enough to talk without waking the camp. The canopy above has cleared, starlight cutting sharp silver lines through the branches. We knock the dark glass bottles together with a solid clink.

"To the watch, Barry!" Hadrik says.

"To survival," I reply, taking a cautious mouthful. The liquid burns like peat and pine sap.

"Since we're both indulging," Hadrik says, pulling a worn deck from his tunic, "how about a proper round of Met-Zek under tavern rules?"

"The bluffing game where every lost hand costs a swig of ale?"

 "The very same!" Hadrik says.

"Deal them out," I say.

We lay the cards out on a flat stump. The interlocking scoring tiers are every bit as Byzantine as I remember. How northern patrons manage these calculations after three flagons remains a mystery, unless drunken chaos is the intended mechanic.

Predictably, my score plummets within four hands. Hadrik plays with practiced ease, bluffing with an iron face. Fortunately for my standing, he grows impatient waiting to lose hands and starts taking swigs on his own turns. As the liquor works on him, his tactical discipline slips, leveling the field—though with the penalty sips mounting on my side, keeping the suit modifiers straight requires serious focus.

"So, Barry," Hadrik says, flicking a card face-up onto the wood. "What's your honest read on Melindra? You had that episode with her in your tent, and outside of Flower, you're the only one she talks to in private. Think she'll ever settle into this squad?"

}
#if(v_b2_ch9_drink == 0) {
 "I'm keeping a clear head tonight," I say. "Just in case scouts test the perimeter."

"Suit yourself," Hadrik shrugs without taking offense. "More for me."

"No objections there," I say.

"Let's take the knoll, then."

After pulling on my gear and strapping on my weapons, I follow his stout frame out into the cool night air. We take up positions on a pair of mossy logs overlooking the tent circle, far enough to talk without waking the camp. The canopy above has cleared, starlight cutting sharp silver lines through the branches. Hadrik uncorks his bottle and raises it in salute.

"To the watch, Barry!" Hadrik says.

"To a quiet night," I answer.

"I was hoping for a bout of Met-Zek under tavern rules," Hadrik says, pulling a worn deck from his tunic, "but since you're abstaining, I'll spot you an opening handicap. Up for a match?"

"Met-zek?" I ask. "The bluffing game from three days ago, where lost hands cost penalty drinks?"

 "The very same!" Hadrik says.

"Deal them out," I say.

We lay the cards out on a flat stump. The interlocking scoring tiers are every bit as Byzantine as I remember. How northern patrons manage these calculations after three flagons remains a mystery, unless drunken chaos is the intended mechanic.

Predictably, my score plummets within four hands. Hadrik plays with practiced ease, bluffing with an iron face. Fortunately for my standing, he grows impatient waiting for lost hands and starts drinking at his own pace. As the liquor works on him, his tactical discipline slips, narrowing the point gap—though closing the distance will take several clean rounds.

"So, Barry," Hadrik says, flicking a card face-up onto the wood. "What's your honest read on Melindra? You had that episode with her in your tent, and outside of Flower, you're the only one she talks to in private. Think she'll ever settle into this squad?"

}
choice("Continue", B2-Ch09b-Interrupt, v_current_scene = B2-Ch09b-Interrupt)


ID: B2-Ch09b-Interrupt
TEXT:

#if(v_b2_ch9_amused == 1 && v_b2_ch8_energizer != 0 && v_b2_ch8_energizer != 2 && v_b2_ch8_energizer != 3) {
 "Doubtful," I tell him, laying a face-up card between us. "I pressed her on it this morning, but she refused to dial back the provocations. Her view is that six centuries of doing whatever she pleases worked fine with Eiden, so there's no reason to accommodate the rest of us."

"Sounds like her," Hadrik sighs.

"Even if she backed off now, the well is already poisoned," I say. "Kate treats her like a live bomb. And the others aren't going out of their way to make friends. Flower is the sole exception."

"I know," Hadrik mutters, scratching his beard. "That was the whole point of dragging them to the card table. Thought if they traded banter over a game, they'd find common ground. Didn't work out as planned."

He checks his hand, a broad grin spreading across his face as he snaps a card face-down onto the wood. Under Met-Zek rules, a player can call an interruption at any stage, forcing a reveal of all face-down cards. Catching a bluff forfeits the round instantly with a quintuple point payout. Calling an honest high card, however, penalizes the challenger fivefold.

The question hangs in the dark: is the dwarf holding gold, or baiting me?

}
#if(v_b2_ch9_amused == 0 && v_b2_ch8_energizer != 0 || v_b2_ch8_energizer == 2 || v_b2_ch8_energizer == 3) {
 "Hard to say," I tell him, laying a face-up card between us. "We talked it over this morning, and she agreed to tone down the baiting, but the damage is already done. Kate doesn't trust a word out of her mouth, and the others aren't going out of their way to make friends. Flower is the sole exception."

"I know," Hadrik mutters, scratching his beard. "That was the whole point of dragging them to the card table. Thought if they traded banter over a game, they'd find common ground. Didn't work out as planned."

He checks his hand, a broad grin spreading across his face as he snaps a card face-down onto the wood. Under Met-Zek rules, a player can call an interruption at any stage, forcing a reveal of all face-down cards. Catching a bluff forfeits the round instantly with a quintuple point payout. Calling an honest high card, however, penalizes the challenger fivefold.

The question hangs in the dark: is the dwarf holding gold, or baiting me?

}
#if(v_b2_ch8_energizer == 0) {
 "Hard to say," I tell him, laying a face-up card between us. "Our relationship hasn't exactly been smooth. We negotiated a temporary truce this afternoon, but the general sentiment in camp remains icy. Kate treats her like a threat, and the others are keeping a wide berth. Flower is the only one giving her the time of day."

"I know," Hadrik mutters, scratching his beard. "That was the whole point of dragging them to the card table. Thought if they traded banter over a game, they'd find common ground. Didn't work out as planned."

He checks his hand, a broad grin spreading across his face as he snaps a card face-down onto the wood. Under Met-Zek rules, a player can call an interruption at any stage, forcing a reveal of all face-down cards. Catching a bluff forfeits the round instantly with a quintuple point payout. Calling an honest high card, however, penalizes the challenger fivefold.

The question hangs in the dark: is the dwarf holding gold, or baiting me?

}
choice("Tell him to turn his card face up", B2-Ch09b-Conversation, v_current_scene = B2-Ch09b-Conversation, v_b2_ch9_flip = 1)
choice("Let the round continue normally", B2-Ch09b-Conversation, v_current_scene = B2-Ch09b-Conversation, v_b2_ch9_flip = 0)


ID: B2-Ch09b-Conversation
TEXT:

set(v_ac_b2_ch9_cards,1) if (v_b2_ch9_drink == 0 && v_b2_ch9_flip == 1)
achievement("Master of cards",v_ac_b2_ch9_cards)
#if(v_b2_ch9_flip == 1) {
 "Turn it over, Hadrik," I tell him.

Hadrik chuckles and flips the card. It's an utter bust—a zero-rank spade that collapses his whole combination. He forfeits the round on the spot, handing me a massive multiplier. Given the grin on his face as he takes another swig from his bottle, he may have bluffed purely for an excuse to drink.

"Sharp eye, Barry!" Hadrik laughs. "Thought I had you second-guessing."

He shuffles the weathered deck with thick, agile fingers, laying out the fresh deal.

"Anyway," Hadrik says, placing his openers, "I'm thinking we call a full party council about Melindra. Leaving this friction to fester until Ollendor is asking for trouble. Infiltrating a walled capital is messy enough without everyone watching their own backs."

"Holding another closed council behind her back will only make it worse," I say. "She made it abundantly clear today how much she hates being excluded. Sidelining her to debate her character just validates every complaint she has."

}
#if(v_b2_ch9_drink == 1 && v_b2_ch9_flip == 0) {
 I let the round continue normally, laying another card face-up.

"Was hoping you'd hold back," Hadrik smirks.

He lays down a matching honor card and flips his face-down play. The revealed card was garbage on its own, but linked to the new play, it triggers a chain multiplier that sweeps the entire board, wiping out my score in one blow.

"Better luck on the next hand, Barry!" Hadrik chuckles, gathering the deck.

"Whatever," I mutter, taking another stiff pull from the ale.

He shuffles and deals out the new round.

"Anyway," Hadrik says, placing his openers, "I'm thinking we call a full party council about Melindra. Leaving this friction to fester until Ollendor is asking for trouble. Infiltrating a walled capital is messy enough without everyone watching their own backs."

"Holding another closed council behind her back will only make it worse," I say. "She made it abundantly clear today how much she hates being excluded. Sidelining her to debate her character just validates every complaint she has."

}
#if(v_b2_ch9_drink == 0 && v_b2_ch9_flip == 0) {
 I let the round continue normally, laying another card face-up.

"Was hoping you'd hold back," Hadrik smirks.

He lays down a matching honor card and flips his face-down play. The revealed card was garbage on its own, but linked to the new play, it triggers a chain multiplier that sweeps the entire board, wiping out my score in one blow.

"Better luck on the next hand, Barry!" Hadrik chuckles, gathering the deck.

"Whatever," I say.

He shuffles and deals out the new round.

"Anyway," Hadrik says, placing his openers, "I'm thinking we call a full party council about Melindra. Leaving this friction to fester until Ollendor is asking for trouble. Infiltrating a walled capital is messy enough without everyone watching their own backs."

"Holding another closed council behind her back will only make it worse," I say. "She made it abundantly clear today how much she hates being excluded. Sidelining her to debate her character just validates every complaint she has."

}
#if(v_b2_ch9_drink == 0 && v_b2_ch9_flip == 1) {
 "Then how do we bridge the gap?" Hadrik asks, scratching his beard.

"We don't," I say. "At least, not with speeches. If a round of conversation could resolve six centuries of bad habits and mutual paranoia, we wouldn't be having this problem. Melindra has to prove herself through actions, not promises. Nothing you or I say will convince Kate anyway."

I tally the cards across the tree stump. The final multiplier carried the remaining trick, pushing my total past the victory threshold.

First time for everything.

"Look at that—a proper Met-Zek win for the novice!" Hadrik beams as the first pale light of dawn bleeds through the pines behind him. "Though we'll have to shelve the victory speech. Company is arriving."

}
#if(v_b2_ch9_drink == 1 || v_b2_ch9_flip == 0) {
 "Then how do we bridge the gap?" Hadrik asks, scratching his beard.

"We don't," I say. "At least, not with speeches. If a round of conversation could resolve six centuries of bad habits and mutual paranoia, we wouldn't be having this problem. Melindra has to prove herself through actions, not promises. Nothing you or I say will convince Kate anyway."

I glance down at the timber, calculating the totals. The trick is already mine, claiming a solid margin on the hand.

"Hand goes to you, Barry!" Hadrik nods as the first pale light of dawn bleeds through the pines behind him. "Though we'll have to hold the next deal. Company is arriving."

}
choice("Continue", B2-Ch09b-Ending, v_current_scene = B2-Ch09b-Ending)


ID: B2-Ch09b-Ending
TEXT:

A heavy, unmasked aura moves through the tree line, broadcasting its approach without the slightest attempt at stealth. The weight of it ranks among the strongest casters in the tournament. A tall, dark-skinned man in his late fifties emerges into the clearing, built like a fortress with cropped gray hair and cold, calculating eyes. Dual broadswords rest across his back, and the supple leather armor over his chest hums with dense layers of enchantment. He steps measuredly over the roots, coming to a halt ten paces from our log.

"Does Daren, the healer in armor, make his camp here?" the stranger asks, his voice measured and deep. "If so, I request an audience."

"You know full well he's in one of those tents," Hadrik replies, rising to his feet and resting a hand on his belt. "Before anyone passes this line, give us a name. Who are you, and what's your business with Daren?"

"Daren neglected to mention me?" A faint, amused expression touches the man's mouth. "A pity. We go back a great many years. We are old companions."

"That's not a name," Hadrik says flatly.

The stranger's smile widens slightly.

"Fair enough," he says. "I am Nolderan. Former sage, master of enchanting, and instructor of martial arts. And I am the man who gave Daren the scar on his forehead."

